  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>Clinique Vétérinaire Vetriviera (the website presents itself under the name Vet Riviera), 448 boulevard du Mercantour in Nice, is the former Saint-Augustin clinic, moved to Saint-Isidore in November 2022 into 1,000 m² premises. It treats dogs, cats and small pets. It is in the Castelli shopping complex, on the 2nd floor, on the route de Grenoble (N202), near the A8 Saint-Isidore motorway exit (Allianz Riviera stadium) and next to the Leclerc; a large free car park of about a hundred spaces, accessible to people with reduced mobility, is available.</p>

      <h2>24/7 emergencies</h2>
      <p>According to the clinic's website, the emergency service is open 24/7 all year round: at night, a vet is present on site at all times and a second is on call for life-saving surgery; on Sundays and public holidays, two vets and three nurses are present. The number is 04 93 71 21 41. The clinic asks you to call before heading over: from 8:30am to 7pm the switchboard answers, outside those hours the call is directed to a veterinary triage service that tells you whether to come immediately or follow advice at home.</p>
      <div class="callout"><svg><use href="#i-check"/></svg> Emergencies: clinic open 24/7 (clinic website). Call 04 93 71 21 41 before you come.</div>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Consultation appointments and information requests are made during switchboard opening hours (8:30am-7pm), so as not to saturate the emergency line; online booking is offered. Procedures carried out at night, at weekends and on public holidays may incur an emergency surcharge; a quote is presented as soon as the animal's condition allows. Payment by bank card, cash or cheque. Owners generally cannot stay in the treatment room during technical procedures.</p>
      <h2>Technical platform</h2>
      <p>The website cites 7 consultation rooms, a waiting room where dogs and cats are separated, a CT scanner, ultrasound, digital X-ray and fibre-optic endoscopy, four operating theatres (including orthopaedics and neurosurgery), an analysis lab, a physiotherapy room and 24/7 hospitalisation when the animal's condition requires it. NAC (small pet) emergencies are not handled at night or at weekends.</p>
      <p>The clinic is certified Cat Friendly Clinic for its cat ward and is a member of the CAP Douleur network, dedicated to animal pain management. For dogs, hospitalisation includes a kennel with large boxes and a small outdoor yard, as well as an intensive-care area; the physiotherapy room is used for rehabilitation after surgery. For emergencies, the website lists in particular ingestion of toxic products (chocolate, rat poison, antifreeze), trauma, seizures, stings with facial swelling and, in dogs, a bloated belly with unproductive retching, which may suggest a twisted stomach.</p>
